  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Moisture from the remnants of TS Iselle\nis streaming into the region today helping drive PW values up to\nan inch plus. Light reflectivity and cloud cover on satellite and\nradar is being observed ahead of an anticipated bigger push of\nprecipitation over the area Friday afternoon. WPC has placed a \nMarginal risk of excessive rainfall over most of the region today,\nexcluding far western Humboldt and southern White Pine Counties. \n0.1-0.25 inches of rainfall is possible over much of the area with\nhigher rain rates possible late Friday afternoon and evening. \nHigher rates increase the risk of debris flows and localized flash\nflooding over recent wildfire burn scars in the area.\n\nShower and thunderstorm coverage will remain through Friday night.\nThe severe potential increases on Saturday though spatial coverage\nof active weather decreases slightly. SPC has added a slight risk\nover eastern NV for wind gusts greater than 57 mph and 1 inch or\nbetter hail. This is in response to robust CAPE (500+ J/kg), bulk\nshear, and helicity values across east central NV. A marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall over eastern NV for Saturday could \nlead to additional rain accumulations reaching half an inch for a \n2 day total. \n\nThe HPC responsible for the recent monsoonal push slides eastward\non Sunday keeping moisture availability restricted to the eastern\nedge of the state over the NV/UT border. With the high to the east\nthe weather pattern will be dominated by the storm track over the\nwestern CONUS mainly to the north of the region. Seasonally\naverage temperatures are forecast to continue into next week.\n\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Moderate confidence remains in\nshower and thunderstorm activity through Friday and Saturday. Low\nconfidence exists in precipitation amounts associated with\nprecipitation this weekend. NBM surprisingly had adequate\nrepresentation of thunderstorm activity for Friday afternoon. No\nmajor grid changes needed at this time.",
